ons), and 肺功能不全 (pulmonary insufficiency). These contraindications stem from the drug’s narrow therapeutic index and potential to exacerbate underlying organ dysfunction or immune compromise. Administering MTX in these populations significantly increases the risk of life-threatening complications, including bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity, and pulmonary toxicity. Regulatory and clinical guidelines uniformly prohibit its use in such contexts to prevent avoidable harm [Data: Relationships (6799, 6800, 6802, 6805)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Drug and food interactions extend MTX’s risk profile beyond direct toxicity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond intrinsic adverse effects, 甲氨蝶呤’s safety is further modulated by interactions with other drugs and dietary components. While the provided data includes a relationship between 他克莫司 (tacrolimus) and 大豆 (soy) discussing food-drug interactions involving CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, it does not directly link soy to MTX. However, the broader context illustrates that common foods and herbal products can alter the metabolism of drugs like tacrolimus via cytochrome P450 pathways—a mechanism relevant to MTX, which is also subject to renal excretion and transporter-mediated clearance. Although no direct soy-MTX link is documented here, the example underscores the complexity of managing MTX in patients consuming diverse diets or concomitant medications [Data: Relationships (28324)]. Notably, sulfonamides and probenecid are documented to increase MTX serum concentrations through protein-binding competition, though only the general phenomenon is cited in the evidence for 血清浓度增高 [Data: Relationships (14236)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Delayed excretion is a critical mechanism underlying MTX toxicity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"MTX排泄延迟 (MTX excretion delay) and the more general 排泄延迟 (excretion delay) are central to the drug’s toxicity profile. Delayed renal elimination leads to prolonged systemic exposure, increasing the likelihood of adverse events such as mucositis and myelosuppression. This delay is often precipitated by co-administered agents like PPIs (e.g., omeprazole), which interfere with renal tubular secretion—likely via inhibition of organic anion transporters. A review of case reports shows that in 9 out of 10 documented instances, PPI co-administration resulted in MTX excretion delay. Monitoring MTX blood levels at 44h and 68h post-dose is standard practice to detect such delays early and initiate rescue therapies like leucovorin [Data: Relationships (14206, 14214, 14237)].\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 8.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to 甲氨蝶呤's association with life-threatening toxicities, narrow therapeutic window, multiple contraindications, and clinically significant drug interactions that can lead to fatal outcomes if not properly managed.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782122062, "model": "qwen3-max", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1581, "prompt_tokens": 7014, "total_tokens": 8595, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1280}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"甲氨蝶呤 (MTX) and Associated Adverse Effects and Interactions\",\n    \"summary\": \"This community centers on the drug 甲氨蝶呤 (methotrexate, MTX), which exhibits numerous relationships with clinical indicators, adverse reactions, contraindicated patient populations, and interacting substances. MTX is associated with serious side effects including gastrointestinal toxicity, mucosal ulceration, delayed excretion, and potentially fatal toxicit